From: any2bazel Ladybird migration
Subject: [PATCH] LibRequests: tear the request down once its body is delivered

WebContent leaks one AF_UNIX socket fd per *completed* HTTP request, so a
long-running browser eventually dies with EMFILE. Ulf's build died overnight
with `dup: Too many open files (errno=24)` at
Libraries/LibWebView/CompositorConnection.cpp:62, which kills three processes
(the MUST there aborts WebContent; the Compositor's VERIFY(connection) at
Services/Compositor/ConnectionFromClient.cpp:68 follows; then ladybird's MUST
in initialize_client).

The leaked fd is RequestServer's response-pipe half (RequestPipe::create,
Services/RequestServer/RequestPipe.cpp:46). RequestServer closes its end;
WebContent never closes its. It is held by a reference cycle that spans the GC
heap and the refcount heap:

  Heap::m_roots -> the four GC::Roots created at Fetch/Fetching/Fetching.cpp:2338
                -> moved onto the refcounted Requests::Request's callbacks
                -> Response holds that Request by RefPtr (Responses.h)
                -> which is reachable from the roots.

Neither collector can break it: the GC sees a live explicit root, and the
refcount never reaches zero. Requests::Request owns the response fd, so the fd
lives as long as the cycle -- i.e. forever.

Request::defer_teardown() is the only thing that clears those callbacks, and its
only callers are stop() and did_transfer(). Normal completion never reaches it,
which is why aborted fetches leak nothing and successful ones leak one fd each.

The obvious one-liner -- defer_teardown() at the end of did_finish() -- is WRONG
and I verified it: the body may still be draining when the request_finished IPC
arrives, so it renders blank pages while making the fd count look perfect. The
correct point is where the stream handler has *already decided* the body is
complete (the user_finish_called / EOF / has_received_all_reported_bytes branch),
which is exactly where the user's finish callback runs.

Measured on a CMake build of f9e34731, WebContent `socket:` fds after 200
fetches: 208 before, 5 after, with the page's own success criteria unchanged
(ok=200 bad=0). Verified `--headless=text` still prints the document text, so
the body is still delivered.

NOTE (scope): this fixes the completed-request class of the leak, identifiable
in `ss -np` by a **dead peer** (`* 0` peer inode: RequestServer already closed
its half). A second, independent class survives this patch: a response whose
body stalls server-side is retained with its peer still ALIVE, because
`on_finish` never runs at all. That one needs the cycle broken at the Response
end and is reported separately; this patch is necessary, not sufficient.
diff --git a/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.cpp b/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.cpp
index 6262425a..7bac6c81 100644
--- a/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.cpp
+++ b/Libraries/LibRequests/Request.cpp
@@ -327,6 +327,10 @@ void Request::set_up_internal_stream_data(DataReceived on_data_available)
         if (!m_internal_stream_data->user_finish_called && (!m_internal_stream_data->read_stream || m_internal_stream_data->read_stream->is_eof() || has_received_all_reported_bytes)) {
             m_internal_stream_data->user_finish_called = true;
             user_on_finish(m_internal_stream_data->total_size, m_internal_stream_data->timing_info, m_internal_stream_data->network_error);
+
+            // The body is fully delivered: drop the callbacks (and the GC::Roots they captured)
+            // and close the response fd, as stop()/did_transfer() do for the abandoned cases.
+            defer_teardown();
         }
     };
 
